      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the certificate.\n\nKey facts:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- OIML Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Certificate signed by RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\n- Date issued: 30 May 2022\n- Page total: 16 pages\n- Applicant: ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA, Via F.lli Gracchi, 27; Cinisello Balsamo; 20 099 Italy\n- Manufacturer: Hemina SPA, Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD; PC: 350 44, Italy\n- Certified type: MS2500 / MV110\n- Module designation: MS2500 / MV110 HV, MS2500 / MV110 LV, MS2500 / MV110 LLV\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013, accuracy classes 1 and 2\n- Type evaluation report: No. 0511-ER-V062-20 dated 19 May 2022, 254 pages including annexes 1-4\n- Test report No. 6015-PT-P5001-22, 94 pages including annexes 1-4\n- Test report No. 6011-PT-SW009-22, 9 pages including annex 1\n- Test report No. 8551-PT-E0041-21, 29 pages including annexes 1\n- Documentation file: 0511-UL-V062-20\n- Revision history: Addition 0, 30 May 2022, Issuing certificate\n\nDescription:\n- Water meters MS2500 / MV110, electromagnetic water meters with electronic indicating device\n- Consist of sensor and converter: sensor MS2500, converter MV110\n- Liner types: PTFE (DN25-DN350, 90°C), Polypropylene (DN25-DN150, 50°C), Rilsan (DN50-DN350, 50°C), Ebonite (DN200-DN350, 50°C)\n- Volume resolution 0.001 m³\n- Power supply variants:\n  1. HV: 100-240V AC\n  2. LV: 24-36V AC/DC\n  3. LLV: 10-48V DC\n\nComponents:\n- Sensor MS2500 (role: sensor / measurement transducer)\n- Converter MV110 (role: converter / electronic indicating device)\n\nModel family: The cert covers variants by power supply: HV, LV, LLV. These are module designations. Should I model them as model variants? The models are MS2500/MV110 HV, MS2500/MV110 LV, MS2500/MV110 LLV. The attributes that vary: power supply voltage.\n\nModel-level characteristics:\n- power_supply_voltage: HV: {min 100, max 240} V; LV: {min 24, max 36} V; LLV: {min 10, max 48} V\n- power_supply_type: HV: \"AC\"; LV: \"AC/DC\"; LLV: \"DC\"\n\nShould I include LV/LLV as separate model entries? Yes, models are MS2500/MV110 HV, etc.\n\nAlso could do config_level for liner types, but that's more like components characteristics. The liner info could be config-level: attribute liner type, axis DN size range and water temperature. Hmm, let me think. Rule 5 says config axes when attributes vary by configuration. Liner options:\n1. PTFE: DN25-DN350, water temp 90°C\n2. Polypropylene: DN25-DN150, 50°C\n3. Rilsan: DN50-DN350, 50°C\n4. Ebonite: DN200-DN350, 50°C\n\nI could express this as config_level entries: attribute \"water_temperature_max\", axis \"liner_type\", values [{condition: \"PTFE\", value: 90, unit: °C}, ...]. And DN range: attribute \"nominal_size_range\" with min/max DN values. Or use matrix_tables for liner options. Maybe a config_level entry:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"maximum_water_temperature\", \"axis\": \"liner_type\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"PTFE\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}, {\"condition\": \"Polypropylene\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}, {\"condition\": \"Rilsan\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}, {\"condition\": \"Ebonite\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}]}\n\nAnd nominal size ranges:\n{\"attribute\": \"nominal_size\", \"axis\": \"liner_type\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"PTFE\", \"value\": {\"min\": 25, \"max\": 350}}, ...]} — unit DN. Unit symbol \"DN\" maybe; value unitless. I'll use unit_symbol: \"DN\" or null. Hmm, DN25-DN350 → value {min: 25, max: 350}, unit_symbol \"DN\". That seems reasonable.\n\nAlso volume_resolution: 0.001 m³ — type_level.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\" (it's R49 water meters). The category examples list \"Gas meter\" etc. This is \"Water meter\".\n\nType designations: MS2500 / MV110. Also mention \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\". type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"] — maybe include the variants too? Rule: type_designations[] — all type/model identifiers mentioned. I'll include [\"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\"]? Hmm, module designations are the variants. Actually the module designation field lists MS2500/MV110 HV, LV, LLV. I think type_designations should include MS2500/MV110 and the module designations could be components or in model_family. Let me put type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"] and model_family models: HV, LV, LLV variants. But the module designations are the whole water meter variants. I'll include them in model_family models with model_id \"MS2500/MV110 HV\" etc.\n\nModule designation: The schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For this cert, the \"Designation of the module\" field is filled with MS2500/MV110 HV etc. Hmm, that's the module designation field but it contains the type names. I'll set module_designation to null or the listed value? The schema expects a module designation like \"Analog load cell\". Here the field content is the variant designations. I could set module_designation: null and capture variants in model_family. Or set it to \"MS2500 / MV110 HV / LV / LLV\". Hmm. I think safest: module_designation: null isn't great since the field is present. But its content is model variants, which I capture in model_family. Actually I could include them in type_designations too. Let me set type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"], and module_designation: null with a note? No notes allowed. I'll set module_designation to \"MS2500 / MV110 HV / MS2500 / MV110 LV / MS2500 / MV110 LLV\"? That's messy. Alternatively, treat the variants as model family and set module_designation: null. Hmm.\n\nActually R49 is a system recommendation; the \"module\" here is the water meter (sensor + converter). I'll go with module_designation: null and put variants into model_family. Actually wait — rule 8: don't fabricate; if a field is present, include it. The field \"Designation of the module (if applicable)\" contains \"MS2500 / MV110 HV / LV / LLV\". I'll record it as module_designation: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV / LV / LLV\"? That's a fabrication of condensed text. Perhaps better: since these are the model variants, they belong in model_family. I'll set module_designation to null? Hmm, the schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Since content is not a module type designation in that sense, null is acceptable. But it loses info... The variants are captured in model_family.models, so info isn't lost. I'll use null.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe include module_designation: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV, MS2500 / MV110 LV, MS2500 / MV110 LLV\"? The rule says don't fabricate, but quoting is not fabricating. Hmm. The model_family captures it. I'll go with null to keep clean, since model_family captures the variants.\n\nHmm, but one could argue the module designation is present. I'll include it as a string listing variants. Actually no — model_family.models covers it. Keep null.\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present — omit\n- page_total: 16\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"30 May 2022\" — maybe ISO \"2022-05-30\". I'll use \"2022-05-30\". Hmm, no strict format rule; \"30 May 2022\" is fine but ISO is cleaner. I'll use \"2022-05-30\".\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present — omit.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"] — address is \"Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\". Split into lines: [\"Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"] or single line. I'll split into [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"].\n- person_responsible: Jan Kalandra. But also the signature block: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. Which is person_responsible? The table says \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". The signature: Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. The schema has person_responsible and person_title. The table explicitly says person responsible: Jan Kalandra (no title given). The signed by is different. I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". Hmm, but maybe person_responsible should be the signer? The table field literally is \"Person responsible\". I'll use Jan Kalandra. Then Pavel Klenovský info — there's no separate field. Could omit, or... schema doesn't have signer fields. I'll omit Klenovský or include? Maybe omit to avoid fabricating schema fields. Hmm, \"person_title\" pairs with person_responsible. Jan Kalandra has no title listed. I'll leave title out. Alternatively include person_responsible \"Pavel Klenovský\" with title \"Head of Certification Body\" since that's the signing authority... The table explicitly labels Jan Kalandra as person responsible though. Keep Jan Kalandra.\n\nApplicants: ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA, address \"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27; Cinisello Balsamo; 20 099 Italy\" → lines: [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo\", \"20 099 Italy\"] — wait, 20099 is the actual CAP for Cinisello Balsamo; OCR shows \"20 099 Italy\". Keep as-is: \"20099 Italy\"? Don't fix; keep \"20 099 Italy\"? I'll keep as printed: \"20 099 Italy\". Hmm, splitting on semicolons: [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo\", \"20 099 Italy\"].\n\nManufacturers: Hemina SPA, \"Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD; PC: 350 44, Italy\" → [\"Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD\", \"PC: 350 44\", \"Italy\"]. Maybe [\"Via Piemonte 1\", \"Montagnana – PD\", \"350 44 Italy\"]. Keep close to original: [\"Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD\", \"PC: 350 44\", \"Italy\"].\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"]\n- module_designation: null (or variants)\n- description: summarize the description. Include the key text. Free-text description. I'll write a concise description: \"Electromagnetic water meter with electronic indicating device, consisting of sensor MS2500 and converter MV110 in compact or separate version; measures, memorises and displays volume of water at metering conditions. Volume resolution 0.001 m³. Available with PTFE, Polypropylene, Rilsan or Ebonite liners; power supply variants HV (100–240 V AC), LV (24–36 V AC/DC), LLV (10–48 V DC).\"\n\nModel family:\n- family_name: null (no explicit family name; MS2500/MV110 could be family name? It's the type designation. I'll set family_name: \"MS2500 / MV110\"? The rule: \"explicit family name if any, else null\". There is no explicit family name. Set null. Actually models[]: model_id: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", label maybe \"High voltage power supply variant\". Attributes: power supply. Hmm label optional. Let me give:\n\nmodels:\n- {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"label\": \"Power supply 100–240 V AC\"}\n- {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"label\": \"Power supply 24–36 V AC/DC\"}\n- {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"label\": \"Power supply 10–48 V DC\"}\n\nattributes: could embed power supply attributes. Rule says models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I'll put attributes: {\"power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {...}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}, \"power_supply_type\": \"AC\"}. But that duplicates model_level. Maybe simpler to keep attributes empty and use model_level. I'll add attributes minimally or omit. I'll include attributes with power supply info for completeness? To avoid duplication, I'll leave attributes out and rely on model_level. Actually schema shows attributes{} — optional. Omit.\n\nComponents:\n- {\"role\": \"sensor\", \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"], \"characteristics\": {...liners...}}\n- {\"role\": \"converter\", \"type_designations\": [\"MV110\"], \"characteristics\": {\"type_level\": {\"volume_resolution\": ...}}}\n\nWait — volume resolution is on the converter's display: \"The water meter displays the volume resolution of 0.001 m3\". It's a whole-meter characteristic; put in type_level of characteristics. Components characteristics: sensor liners could be component characteristics. The schema's components characteristics{} — free-form. I'll put liner info in the sensor component characteristics or in config_level. Let me use config_level for liner as decided, plus maybe matrix. I think config_level is right:\n\nconfig_level:\n1. {\"attribute\": \"nominal_size_range\", \"axis\": \"liner_type\", \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"PTFE\", \"value\": {\"min\": 25, \"max\": 350}, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Polypropylene\", \"value\": {\"min\": 25, \"max\": 150}, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Rilsan\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 350}, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Ebonite\", \"value\": {\"min\": 200, \"max\": 350}, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"}\n ]}\n2. {\"attribute\": \"maximum_water_temperature\", \"axis\": \"liner_type\", \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"PTFE\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Polypropylene\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Rilsan\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Ebonite\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n ]}\n\nHmm, the config values should follow the structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol} — but should value be the structured object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}? Rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" So config values have condition, value, unit_symbol. But should value be nested with unit_symbol? Rule 3's structured value shape is {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For config_level, the example in rule 5 shows {condition, value, unit_symbol} — flattened. I'll use {condition, value: <structured or raw>, unit_symbol}. To be safe, use the same shape as model_level values but with condition: {\"condition\": \"PTFE\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null}. Hmm. Model-level values in the R137 example: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no unit per value. For config, rule 5 example: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\". I'll do {\"condition\": \"PTFE\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}.\n\nFor model_level values, follow R137 example: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ...}. Add unit at attribute level via \"unit_symbol\". Should I add footnote_markers where applicable? Not needed here.\n\nmodel_level:\n1. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [\n   {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}},\n   {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 24, \"max\": 36}},\n   {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 48}}\n ]}\n2. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_type\", \"values\": [\n   {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"value\": \"AC\"},\n   {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"value\": \"AC/DC\"},\n   {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"value\": \"DC\"}\n ]}\n\ntype_level:\n- volume_resolution: {value: 0.001, unit_symbol: \"m³\", unit_id: null}. Could also be \"u:litre\"? Keep null. unit_symbol \"m³\".\n\ncharacteristics.type_level: {\"volume_resolution\": {\"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", \"unit_id\": null}}\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical, e.g. \"R60\")\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: omit (not present)\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"] or [1, 2]? \"For accuracy class (if applicable): 1 and 2\". Accuracy classes could be numeric. R49 classes 1 and 2. I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"]? Hmm. Schema just says accuracy_classes[]. Numbers seem fine: [1, 2]. But class identifiers are often strings like \"C\", \"III\". Here they're numbers. I'll use [1, 2]? Or [\"1\", \"2\"]. I'll go with [\"1\", \"2\"]? Hmm. Let me think — for load cells classes are like \"C3\". Here \"1 and 2\". I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"] to be safe as identifiers. Actually numbers are more structured. Either acceptable. I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"].\n\ntest_reports:\n- {\"id\": \"0511-ER-V062-20\", \"date\": \"2022-05-19\", \"pages\": 254, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {\"id\": \"6015-PT-P5001-22\", \"pages\": 94, \"role\": \"test_report\"} (no date)\n- {\"id\": \"6011-PT-SW009-22\", \"pages\": 9, \"role\": \"test_report\"}\n- {\"id\": \"8551-PT-E0041-21\", \"pages\": 29, \"role\": \"test_report\"}\n\nShould test_reports include prefix \"No.\"? id: \"6015-PT-P5001-22\".\n\ndocumentation file: 0511-UL-V062-20 → test_reports with role \"documentation_file\": {\"id\": \"0511-UL-V062-20\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2022-05-30\", \"changes\": \"Issuing certificate\"} — the revision is \"Addition 0\". Rule: revision string e.g. \"0\". The table says \"Addition 0\". I'll put revision: \"0\" and changes \"Issuing certificate\"? Or revision \"Addition 0\"? The rule example: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). The literal value is \"Addition 0\". I'll use \"0\" and mention in changes? Hmm, \"Addition 0\" is CMI's style meaning first issue. I'll put revision: \"Addition 0\"? The rule says string e.g. \"0\" not 0 — it's about type, not content. I'll use \"0\" with changes \"Issuing certificate\". Hmm, to be faithful: revision \"Addition 0\". I'll go with \"0\" — no wait, faithful extraction: \"Addition 0\". Let me keep \"Addition 0\"? The schema example \"0\" suggests extracting the number as string. I'll use \"0\". Hmm... Actually CMI certificates use \"Addition 0\" as the revision label. Faithful is better: \"Addition 0\". I'll go with \"0\"... \n\nDecision: use \"0\" since the rule exemplifies \"0\". But \"Addition 0\" preserves info. Risky either way; I'll use \"Addition 0\" — no. Ugh. The rule: `revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0)` — emphasizes string type. Content should be what's in the doc: \"Addition 0\". I'll use \"Addition 0\". Hmm, but that could break consumers expecting numeric-ish strings. I'll compromise: revision: \"0\", changes: \"Issuing certificate\". And maybe add the word addition into changes: \"Issuing certificate (Addition 0)\"? That's fabrication-ish. I'll just use \"0\".\n\nActually, let me keep it simple and faithful: \"revision\": \"0\". Fine.\n\nfootnotes: none present.\n\nmatrix_tables: none needed (liner options could be a matrix table but config_level handles it).\n\ncertificate.page_total: 16 (\"Page 1 of 16 pages\").\n\nDate format: The instructions don't mandate ISO. I'll use ISO dates: \"2022-05-30\" and \"2022-05-19\".\n\nOutput JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 16,\n    \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2022-05-30\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo\", \"20 099 Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Hemina SPA\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD\", \"PC: 350 44\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500 / MV110\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"label\": \"Mains power supply 100–240 V AC\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"label\": \"Mains power supply 24–36 V AC/DC\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"label\": \"Power supply 10–48 V DC\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\"role\": \"sensor (measurement transducer)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"], \"characteristics\": {\"type_level\": {\"liner_options\": ...}}},\n    {\"role\": \"converter (electronic indicating device)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MV110\"]}\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"volume_resolution\": {\"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", \"unit_id\": null}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\", \"2\"]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...]\n}\n\nComponents characteristics: For sensor, liners: characteristics: {\"liners\": [\"PTFE\", \"Polypropylene\", \"Rilsan\", \"Ebonite\"]}? Free-form. Or omit since config_level covers it. I'll add a simple note-free listing: \"liner_types\": [\"PTFE\", \"Polypropylene\", \"Rilsan\", \"Ebonite\"]. And converter: communication interfaces impulse output, current output, RS485, USB mini-B. Could add \"communication_interfaces\": [\"impulse output\", \"current output\", \"RS485\", \"USB mini-B\"]. These are raw strings, fine as component characteristics.\n\nDescription text: compose from markdown. Keep it reasonably complete.\n\nFor the module_designation field — I decided null. Hmm, but let me reconsider once more. The document has a field \"Designation of the module (if applicable)\" with values \"MS2500 / MV110 HV / LV / LLV\". These are actually whole-meter variant designations, captured as model_family.models. Setting module_designation to null loses that field's presence but not the info. I'll go with null.\n\nActually — maybe better: since \"module\" in OIML-CS terms for WELMEC... For R49 water meter, sensor MS2500 + converter MV110 could be considered modules. The field content though lists HV/LV/LLV variants. I'll keep null and rely on model_family.\n\nOne more consideration: should components alternatives be AND? Sensor MS2500 AND converter MV110 — they're both named. The schema has alternatives field for OR lists. Since the meter consists of sensor AND converter together, alternatives: \"AND\"? The rule: when cert offers alternatives, record OR. Here they're both required. I could set alternatives: \"AND\" for each? That seems off. I'll omit alternatives.\n\nConfig axis name: \"liner_type\". Attribute names: \"nominal_size_range\" with DN units, \"maximum_water_temperature\".\n\nFor nominal_size_range values, unit_symbol \"DN\" — value {min, max}. In config values shape {condition, value, unit_symbol}: {\"condition\": \"PTFE (liner)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 25, \"max\": 350}, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"}.\n\nWait — should condition just be \"PTFE\"? Yes.\n\nmodel_level power supply: HV \"has power supply by main 100 - 240V AC\". So voltage 100–240 V AC. LV: 24–36 V AC/DC. LLV: 10–48 V DC.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"V\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}},\n    {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 24, \"max\": 36}},\n    {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 48}}\n  ]\n},\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_type\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"value\": \"AC\"},\n    {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"value\": \"AC/DC\"},\n    {\"model\": \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"value\": \"DC\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nModel ids: use \"MS2500/MV110 HV\" etc. (from module designation). Good.\n\nAlso the model_id in models[] should match model_level \"model\" strings. Yes.\n\npage_total: 16.\n\nInclude project_number? Not present. oiml_issuer_id? Not present — omit.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nNow write the description: \n\n\"Electromagnetic water meter designed to measure, memorise and display the volume of water at metering conditions, consisting of a sensor (MS2500) and a converter (MV110) in compact or separate version with connecting cable. The sensor has a steel body with flanges, one pair of excitation coils and electrodes, and can be equipped with PTFE, polypropylene, Rilsan or ebonite liners. The converter has plastic/aluminium housing with LCD display showing volume and flow, volume resolution 0.001 m³, three control buttons, impulse and current outputs; non-legally-relevant communication via RS485 or USB mini-B. Security levels via passwords. Available in HV, LV and LLV power supply variants; can be installed in any position.\"\n\nGood.\n\nFinal JSON. Ensure valid JSON, no markdown fences.",
